---
<SiteLayout title="Case III Recoveries" activeSection="manual" activeSubject="naval-operations"
 compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Naval Operations',href:sitePath('manual/naval-operations/')},{label:'Case III Recoveries'}]}>
 <section class="status-panel">
  <h2>{recoveryModule.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {recoveryModule.revision} · Conventional fixed-wing, arrested carrier recoveries. Aircraft-agnostic concepts, illustrated using historical US Navy training material. Helicopter and vertical-landing procedures are outside this module.</p>
  <p>Read the recovery sequence, explore the navigation geometry, then practise selected skills in the optional standalone Tomcat exercise.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <ol>{contents.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
 </nav>
 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>Case III provides an instrument recovery to the carrier at night and in weather requiring instrument procedures. It connects a sequenced marshal hold to an instrument approach and the final visual landing task. The general controller-training text assigns selection of the recovery case to the Air Officer; the P-816 account calls this role the Air Boss. <a href="#source-general">[2, p. 11-21]</a> <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-3–2-4, 2-22–2-25]</a></p>
 <p>By the end, you should be able to explain the recovery phases; distinguish a radial, inbound course, <a href={term('Base Recovery Course (BRC)')}>base recovery course</a> and <a href={term('Final Bearing')}>final bearing</a>; interpret a <a href={term('Marshal')}>marshal</a> clearance; explain why timing and configuration matter; and distinguish a landing, <a href={term('Bolter')}>bolter</a> and <a href={term('Waveoff')}>waveoff</a>.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a conceptual reference, not an aircraft checklist. Exact speeds, configuration, approach aids, minima and missed-approach instructions depend on the aircraft, equipment, applicable procedure and clearance. The source is a T-45C training publication: its aircraft actions are not universal.</p>
 <h2 id="picture">The whole recovery at a glance</h2>
 <p>Figure 1 follows one aircraft through the historical CV-1 Case III example in [1], from the first check-in to the ball call, with the bolter or waveoff re-entry. Select a number to highlight that part of the figure. The numbered text is the explanation; the figure only places it. Numbers such as 1,200 ft or 150 kt belong to that T-45C training example, not to every aircraft.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
  <h3 id="overview-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — Case III recovery overview (plan view)</h3>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><RecoveryOverview /></div>
  <ol class="callouts">
   <li data-callout-item="1"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Red Crown, then Strike</button><p>After the mission, check in with Red Crown, the carrier strike group's air defence controller, giving call sign, position and altitude. Red Crown passes instructions and hands you to Strike; that handoff should occur before you enter the 50 NM carrier control area (CCA). The Strike check-in adds the flight's low fuel state. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 1-3, 2-11]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="2"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Marshal check-in</button><p>Once inside the CCA, Strike hands you to Marshal. Check in with call sign, position, altitude and low state. Marshal assigns the recovery case and holding instructions, including altitude, and passes weather, altimeter, BRC and bingo information. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-12]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="3"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">The marshal fix</button><p>In this source the fix is ideally on the 180 radial relative to BRC and generally within 30° of it. Its DME is the assigned altitude in angels plus 15: angels 8 (8,000 ft) gives 23 DME. The lowest assignment for jet and turboprop aircraft is 6,000 ft. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-19]</a> The general controller text references its 180 radial to the expected final bearing instead, so read the radial from your clearance. <a href="#source-general">[2, p. 11-33]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="4"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Holding</button><p>A six-minute left-hand pattern of two-minute turns and one-minute legs. Aircraft in the stack are separated by 1,000 ft and must be established at their assigned altitude by 10 NM from the stack. Arrive at the fix on airspeed (250 kt) ready to commence at EAT plus or minus 10 seconds, and tell Marshal when established. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-20, 2-26]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="5"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Commence</button><p>Leave the fix at EAT and call “commencing” with the altimeter setting and fuel state. Establish a 4,000 ft/min descent at 250 KIAS. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-23, 2-27]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="6"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Handoff to Approach</button><p>Marshal hands you to the final controller during the penetration, ideally before platform. In the source dialogue the call is “switch Approach, button 18”, and you check in on the new frequency with your fuel state. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-26–2-27]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="7"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Platform</button><p>At 5,000 ft, normally about 20 NM from the ship, reduce the descent to 2,000 ft/min and report “platform”. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-23, 2-27, A-5]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="8"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Correct to final bearing</button><p>If the marshal radial is not the reciprocal of the final bearing, correct at 20 DME: gradually when the final bearing is within 10° of that reciprocal, otherwise with a 30° correction. If not established on final bearing by 12 miles, fly the 12-mile arc until you intercept it. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="9"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Level off and configure</button><p>Level at 1,200 ft. In the T-45C example, landing checks start at 10 DME, the gear is down no later than 8 DME, and the aircraft crosses 6 DME at 150 kt before slowing to final approach speed. Configuration and speeds come from your own aircraft's procedure. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="10"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">ACLS lock-on</button><p>ACLS lock-on occurs between 8 and 4 DME. Approach asks you to “say needles” and compares your reply with the radar before directing “fly your needles”. If they disagree, the controller breaks lock and directs “fly the bullseye” (ICLS) until a new lock. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="11"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Call the ball</button><p>At three-quarters of a mile the controller instructs you to call the ball. Transition from the instrument scan to the optical landing cue while the approach is still monitored; the LSO answers “Roger ball”. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-25, 2-27]</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout-item="12"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Bolter or waveoff</button><p>Climb as the procedure directs, turn downwind when instructed by Approach, do the landing checks, report fuel state abeam the ship and expect a turn back to final 4–8 NM past abeam. The figure does not say which side the downwind is on; Approach assigns it. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-25]</a></p></li>
  </ol>
 </section>
 <h2 id="sequence">Recovery sequence</h2>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Recovery sequence" tabindex="0"><table>
 <caption>Conceptual sequence based on [1], §204. Read the phase explanations before the exercise.</caption>
 <thead><tr><th scope="col">Phase</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Understand before continuing</th></tr></thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr><th scope="row">Arrival</th><td>Receive the recovery plan.</td><td>Clearance, weather, fuel and available approach.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Marshal</th><td>Separate and sequence arrivals.</td><td>Fix, altitude, holding pattern and expected approach time.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Commence</th><td>Leave marshal in the assigned sequence.</td><td>Time, inbound course and descent profile.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Penetration</th><td>Descend toward the instrument final.</td><td>Platform, level-off and correction to final bearing.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Final</th><td>Follow approach guidance, then transition to landing cues.</td><td>Configuration, guidance validity and landing signal officer calls.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Recovery outcome</th><td>Arrest or safely re-enter the recovery.</td><td>Applicable bolter/waveoff procedure and controller instructions.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <h2 id="clearance">Arrival, handoffs and marshal clearance</h2>
 <p>The general Navy controller-training text lists the recovery case, approach type, expected final bearing, altimeter, marshal instructions, expected approach time (EAT), approach frequency and time check, with vector/stack information as needed. <a href="#source-general">[2, p. 11-32]</a> The P-816 training example also includes weather and relevant divert/fuel information, and reports when established and commencing. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-26–2-27]</a> Read the clearance as a connected plan: <em>where</em> to wait, <em>at what altitude</em>, <em>when</em> to leave, and <em>which approach</em> follows.</p>
 <p>Fuel planning remains active throughout the recovery. A delayed approach consumes fuel; a bolter or waveoff adds another circuit. The module does not prescribe a common fuel ladder: the source explicitly makes bingo fuel dependent on aircraft type. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. A-1]</a></p>
 <section class="lesson bleed" data-lesson="stack" aria-labelledby="stack-title">
  <h3 id="stack-title">Practise: decode a marshal clearance</h3>
  <p>This is Marshal's reply to aircraft 405 in the source's Case III dialogue. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-26]</a> Select each phrase to see what it gives you. The figure shows where that places you in the stack.</p>
  <p class="clearance" data-clearance aria-label="Example clearance, phrase by phrase"></p>
  <p class="feedback" data-meaning role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <dl class="decode">
   <dt>405,</dt><dd>The call sign: this clearance is for aircraft 405 only. Case III recoveries are flown as single aircraft in this source. [1, p. 2-22]</dd>
   <dt>Mother's weather is 600 overcast, visibility 3 miles,</dt><dd>The ship's weather. “Mother” is the ship. [1, p. 1-10]</dd>
   <dt>altimeter 29.87.</dt><dd>The altimeter setting. Read it back; it is updated later in the same dialogue.</dd>
   <dt>Case III recovery, CV-1 approach.</dt><dd>The recovery case and the approach that follows marshal.</dd>
   <dt data-angels="7">Marshal on the 160, 22, angels 7.</dt><dd>The holding fix: the 160 radial at 22 DME, at angels 7. In the source's rule the fix DME is the angels plus 15, so 7 + 15 = 22. [1, p. 2-19]</dd>
   <dt>Expected final bearing 015,</dt><dd>The landing-area centreline direction to expect. The reciprocal of the 160 radial is 340°, which differs from 015° by 35°, so this example would need the 30° correction at 20 DME. [1, p. 2-23]</dd>
   <dt>expected approach time 22.</dt><dd>EAT: the time to leave the fix and commence, to within 10 seconds in this source. [1, p. 2-20]</dd>
   <dt>Approach button 18.</dt><dd>“Button” is the frequency selection for the approach controller you will be handed to. [1, p. 2-21]</dd>
  </dl>
  <div class="lesson-grid wide">
   <div class="controls">
    <label>Assigned altitude <output data-stack-value>Angels 7: 7,000 ft at 22 DME</output><input name="angels" type="range" min="6" max="14" step="1" value="7" /></label>
    <label>Marshal radial (°)<input name="radial" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="160" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
    <label>Final bearing (°)<input name="fb" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="15" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
    <p class="feedback" data-correction role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
   </div>
   <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 2, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg viewBox="0 0 720 310" class="natops" role="img" aria-label="Marshal stack side view"></svg></div>
  </div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Figure 2 — Marshal stack, side view. Slots at angels 6–14 follow the source's angels + 15 rule; the dashed line is the CV-1 example descent in Figure 4. Vertical spacing is exaggerated.</p>
  <noscript><p>The decoder and stack figure need JavaScript. The phrase list above gives every meaning.</p></noscript>
 </section>
 <h2 id="geometry">Bearings and the landing area</h2>
 <dl>
 <dt><strong>Radial</strong></dt><dd>A bearing <em>from</em> the navigation station. Radial plus range locates a fix; it is not a heading from your present position to that fix.</dd>
 <dt><strong>Inbound course</strong></dt><dd>The reciprocal direction toward the station along a radial. The reciprocal of 220° is 040°.</dd>
 <dt><strong>Base recovery course (BRC)</strong></dt><dd>The carrier's magnetic heading for recovery.</dd>
 <dt><strong>Final bearing</strong></dt><dd>The magnetic direction of the landing-area centreline. The angled landing area means this differs from BRC.</dd>
 </dl>
 <figure class="deck-fig">
  <svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 520 170" role="img" aria-labelledby="deck-fig-title">
   <title id="deck-fig-title">Ship heading along BRC; the angled landing area's centreline, extended aft, is the final bearing</title>
   <path class="shipfill" d="M300 70 L430 70 L470 85 L430 100 L300 100 Z" />
   <path class="deck" d="M312 97 L420 72" />
   <path class="ln" d="M300 85 L500 85" marker-end="url(#deck-arrow)" />
   <path class="ln centreline" d="M40 160 L312 97" />
   <defs><marker id="deck-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="currentColor" /></marker></defs>
   <text x="440" y="78" class="lbl">BRC</text>
   <text x="44" y="146" class="lbl">FINAL BEARING: LANDING-AREA CENTRELINE EXTENDED AFT</text>
   <text x="300" y="124" class="lbl small">ANGLED LANDING AREA</text>
   <text x="300" y="30" class="lbl small">ANGLE EXAGGERATED · NOT TO SCALE</text>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>Figure 3 — BRC and final bearing. The ship steams on BRC; you land along the angled landing area, so your final bearing is the direction of its centreline. [1, pp. A-1, A-3; 2, p. 11-32]</figcaption>
 </figure>
 <p>The first two definitions describe the geometry used below; BRC and final bearing are defined in <a href="#source-p816">[1, glossary pp. A-1, A-3]</a>. The general Navy controller text also defines final bearing as the extended landing-area centreline. <a href="#source-general">[2, p. 11-32]</a> Do not substitute BRC for final bearing or assume the marshal radial is always reciprocal to either. The published CV-1 example explicitly provides for correction from the marshal radial to final bearing. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p>
 <h2 id="tacan">Navigate to the fix</h2>
 <p>TACAN (tactical air navigation) provides bearing and DME (distance measuring equipment) range. Position yourself and the fix on a station-centred plot, then find the direction from your position to the fix. Flying the assigned radial as a heading does not solve this point-to-point problem.</p>
 <p>In this educational calculation, bearings are magnetic, north is up and range is in nautical miles (NM). We use a flat plane, horizontal range and no wind; actual DME is slant range. Heading equals track only under the no-wind assumption. The fix is constrained to lie opposite BRC for this exercise; actual clearance geometry can differ.</p>
  <section class="lesson" data-lesson="navigation" aria-labelledby="nav-title">
    <h3 id="nav-title">Practise: heading to the marshal fix</h3>
    <p>Set the ship’s BRC and your own position, estimate the heading to the fix, then check it. The fix is on the BRC + 180° radial for this geometry exercise.</p>
    <div class="lesson-grid">
      <div class="controls">
        <label>BRC (°)<input name="brc" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="0" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <p class="derived">Marshal radial <strong data-fix-radial>180</strong>°</p>
        <label>Fix DME (NM)<input name="fixDme" type="number" min="1" max="60" step="1" value="21" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your radial (°)<input name="youRadial" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="90" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your DME (NM)<input name="youDme" type="number" min="0" max="80" step="1" value="30" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your heading estimate (°)<input name="guess" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <div class="buttons">
          <button type="button" data-check>Check estimate</button>
          <button type="button" data-reveal>Reveal</button>
          <button type="button" data-new>New problem</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 320" role="img" aria-label="Compass plot centred on Mother"></svg>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p data-result aria-live="polite">Worked example: from the 090 radial at 30 DME to the 180 radial at 21 DME, the direct track is about 235° for 36.6 NM.</p>
    <noscript><p>The interactive plot needs JavaScript. The worked example above shows the same calculation.</p></noscript>
  </section>
  <details>
    <summary>How the check is calculated</summary>
    <p>Place Mother at the origin with x east and y north. A position on radial <var>r</var> at <var>d</var> NM is x = <var>d</var>·sin <var>r</var>, y = <var>d</var>·cos <var>r</var>. The track from you to the fix is the direction of the difference vector, atan2(Δx, Δy), and the distance is its length. In the worked example you are at (30, 0) and the fix at (0, −21), so Δx = −30, Δy = −21: the track is 180° + atan(30/21) ≈ 235.0° and the distance √1341 ≈ 36.6 NM. With no wind, that track is also the heading. The time shown uses a fixed educational map rate of 250 NM/hour, which is not a groundspeed model.</p>
  </details>


 <h2 id="holding">Marshal and timing</h2>
 <p>Marshal is a sequencing and separation system, not just an orbit near the ship. The assigned radial, range and altitude define your place in it. Plan to arrive at the fix ready to commence at EAT. Reaching the right position at the wrong time loses the intended sequence. The source describes managing the hold to reach the fix on time and reporting a time discrepancy. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-19–2-20]</a></p>
 <p>The general controller-training text describes a six-minute left-hand jet/turboprop marshal pattern. <a href="#source-general">[2, p. 11-33]</a> The historical pattern in [1] gives its timing as: two two-minute turns plus two one-minute legs. Holding speed is tied to fuel conservation or the aircraft’s specified holding speed. These are the source's documented parameters, not a universal four-minute instrument hold or a common airspeed for every aircraft. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-20]</a></p>
 <p><strong>Timing check:</strong> two turns × 2 min + two legs × 1 min = 6 min. Two circuits = 12 min. That arithmetic explains a pattern's duration; it does not authorise arbitrary leg extensions or leaving a clearance early. Confirm an amended plan with the controller when the assigned timing cannot be met.</p>
 <h2 id="entry">Joining the hold</h2>
 <p>The source sets the conditions for joining marshal rather than a turn technique: be established at the assigned altitude by 10 NM from the stack, and reach the fix on airspeed ready to commence at EAT plus or minus 10 seconds. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-20]</a> It does not describe an entry turn for the pattern. A general instrument technique for joining any racetrack is the FAA's three holding entries. <a href="#source-aim">[3, 5-3-8 j 3]</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Direct:</strong> fly to the fix and turn to follow the pattern.</li>
  <li><strong>Teardrop:</strong> at the fix, turn outbound to a heading 30° from the outbound course toward the holding side, fly it for one minute, then turn in the direction of the hold to intercept the inbound course.</li>
  <li><strong>Parallel:</strong> at the fix, turn to parallel the outbound course on the non-holding side for one minute, then turn in the direction of the hold through more than 180° to return to the fix or intercept the inbound course.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>The holding side is the side of the inbound course where the pattern lies: left of it for the left-hand marshal pattern. The AIM calls a left-turn pattern nonstandard and orients its entries to the 70° line on the holding side, as for a right-hand pattern. <a href="#source-aim">[3, 5-3-8 j 3 (e)]</a> The AIM assigns each entry to a sector of its entry figure, which this module has not verified, so <strong>the practice does not tell you which entry a heading calls for</strong>. It flies the entry you pick so you can see its shape and turns.</p>
 <p class="note"><strong>Applicability:</strong> these are FAA civil instrument holding entries, practised here as a general instrument skill. They are not a Navy marshal requirement in [1].</p>
 <section class="lesson" data-lesson="entry" aria-labelledby="entry-title">
  <h3 id="entry-title">Practise: fly an entry</h3>
  <p>Set the hold, the inbound course and your heading at the fix, then pick an entry. The plot draws the racetrack, your arrival and the flown path. It is a no-wind sketch at 250 kt. The racetrack uses the source's two-minute 180° turns and one-minute legs. The entry turns use the AIM's holding turn: 3° per second, 30° of bank, or 25° of bank with a flight director, whichever needs the least bank. <a href="#source-aim">[3, 5-3-8 j 8]</a> At 250 kt, 3° per second would need about 34° of bank, so the 30° limit applies; a coordinated level turn at 30° of bank is g·tan 30° ÷ speed ≈ 2.5° per second. The flight-director option is not modelled. The plot turns the short way where the AIM names no direction, and cuts the inbound course at no more than 45°, an angle the AIM does not give. Legs stay at one minute. Where the turn back is too wide to roll out on the course, the path crosses it and corrects; where the entry does not line up by the fix, it corrects on the first circuit of the racetrack, as in any hold.</p>
  <div class="lesson-grid">
   <div class="controls">
    <fieldset class="inline">
     <legend>Hold direction</legend>
     <label><input type="radio" name="turns" value="left" checked /> Left (marshal)</label>
     <label><input type="radio" name="turns" value="right" /> Right</label>
    </fieldset>
    <label>Inbound course <output data-inbound-value>000°</output><input name="inbound" type="range" min="0" max="350" step="10" value="0" /></label>
    <label>Heading at the fix <output data-heading-value>090°</output><input name="heading" type="range" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="90" /></label>
    <fieldset class="inline">
     <legend>Entry to fly</legend>
     <label><input type="radio" name="entry" value="direct" /> Direct</label>
     <label><input type="radio" name="entry" value="teardrop" /> Teardrop</label>
     <label><input type="radio" name="entry" value="parallel" checked /> Parallel</label>
    </fieldset>
    <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Random heading</button></div>
   </div>
   <svg viewBox="0 0 320 320" role="img" aria-label="Holding pattern at the marshal fix with the flown entry"></svg>
  </div>
  <p data-result aria-live="polite">Worked example: left-hand hold, inbound course 000°, arriving on heading 090°. The pattern lies west of the inbound course. A parallel entry turns right 90° onto the outbound heading 180°, east of the inbound course on the non-holding side. After one minute it turns left through 225°, more than 180°, to cut the inbound course at 45°. It reaches the fix about 0.5 NM off the inbound course, so it corrects on the first circuit and is established inbound on the next pass.</p>
  <noscript><p>The interactive plot needs JavaScript. The worked example above describes the same entry.</p></noscript>
 </section>

 <h2 id="turns">Timed turns on the turn needle</h2>
 <p>Holding is flown by the clock, so its turns are flown at a known rate. In the source's pattern each 180° turn takes two minutes: 180° ÷ 120 s = 1.5° per second. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-20]</a> A turn needle shows the rate of turn directly, so you can hold a rate without chasing the heading. Heading change = rate × time; time = heading change ÷ rate.</p>
 <p>The scale differs between instruments. The DCS F-14 manual by Heatblur states that one needle deflection corresponds to a 360° turn in four minutes: 360° ÷ 240 s = 1.5° per second, the same rate as the marshal turn. <a href="#source-heatblur">[4]</a> That is simulator documentation for the DCS F-14 module, not the aircraft's NATOPS. For comparison, the FAA's civil holding turns are 3° per second, 30° of bank or 25° of bank with a flight director, whichever needs the least bank. <a href="#source-aim">[3, 5-3-8 j 8]</a></p>
