 <h2 id="penetration">Commence and platform</h2>
 <p>Commencing marks the transition from holding to the approach. The penetration combines descent, course management and controller handoff. Platform is the reporting point in the approach where the descent rate changes. The source glossary places it normally at 5,000 ft and about 20 NM from the ship, so it is both an altitude and a position. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. A-5]</a> The published CV-1 profile in [1] uses 250 KIAS and 4,000 ft/min initially, reducing descent at 5,000 ft to 2,000 ft/min, then levelling at 1,200 ft. These values illustrate that source's profile, not aircraft limitations. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p>
 <p>Keep lateral and vertical tasks separate: descending correctly does not establish you on final bearing. When the marshal radial is offset, the source provides an intercept/arc procedure. Use the actual published approach and clearance rather than steering directly at the ship or applying a fixed deck-angle subtraction. Handoff should ideally occur before platform; check in on the assigned frequency. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-23, 2-26]</a></p>
 <h2 id="profile">The approach profile</h2>
 <p>Figure 4 joins the source's numbers into one side view: commence at the fix, 4,000 ft/min to platform, 2,000 ft/min to 1,200 ft, level flight, then the self-contained GCA check altitudes to the ship. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-19, 2-23, 2-25]</a> Choose a marshal altitude and fly the aircraft down the profile, or drag the position slider.</p>
 <p>The profile is a simplified educational model. It assumes no wind and a groundspeed equal to the 250 kt figure, so each minute covers 250 ÷ 60 ≈ 4.17 NM. A 4,000 ft/min descent then loses 4,000 ÷ 4.17 = 960 ft per NM, and 2,000 ft/min loses 480 ft per NM. From angels 7 at 22 DME, the 2,000 ft down to platform takes 2,000 ÷ 960 ≈ 2.08 NM, reaching platform at about 19.9 DME. The next 3,800 ft at 480 ft per NM takes about 7.92 NM, levelling at about 12.0 DME. Rate changes are instant, and the GCA check altitudes are joined as a straight 400 ft per NM line. True airspeed above sea level is higher than 250 KIAS, so a real aircraft covers more distance in each minute.</p>
