 <h2 id="final">Final approach and the ball</h2>
 <p>Arrive configured and ready for the final approach, using the aircraft's landing checks and limits. Gear, flaps, approach speed and angle of attack belong to the aircraft procedure. The Tomcat's controls and exercise gates are not module-wide rules.</p>
 <p>The source describes automatic carrier landing system (ACLS) lock-on and comparison with instrument carrier landing system (ICLS) indications before following ACLS needles. Those exchanges require that equipment and a valid lock; the words “fly needles” and “fly bullseye” are not interchangeable generic commands. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p>
 <p>The ball is the optical landing system's glideslope cue. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 1-11]</a> In the source's example, the controller requests the ball call at three-quarter mile. The pilot transitions from an instrument scan to outside landing cues while the landing signal officer (LSO) monitors the final landing task. Continue to monitor the approach during this transition. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-25]</a></p>
