 <h2 id="outcomes">Landing, bolter and waveoff</h2>
 <p>The historical training account distinguishes arrestment through engagement of the arresting gear from a bolter, a touchdown without arresting-hook engagement. A waveoff discontinues the landing attempt. The LSO can direct it by radio or signal it with the waveoff lights. Compliance with either signal is mandatory in the source procedure. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-17, A-1]</a></p>
 <p>In the historical Case III training example, recovery continues under instrument approach control after a bolter or waveoff. The source describes climbing, a controller-directed downwind, landing checks, an abeam fuel-state report and another turn to final. That example uses an instrument re-entry rather than its Case I visual circuit. Aircraft-specific climb, speed, gear/flap actions and lost-communication procedures must come from the applicable procedure; this module does not generalise the T-45C instructions to other aircraft. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-25–2-26]</a></p>
 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>Before practising, explain these without looking back:</p>
 <details><summary>Why are BRC and final bearing different?</summary><p>BRC describes ship heading; final bearing describes the angled landing-area centreline. Use the assigned final bearing.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Does 21 DME mean you are at the marshal fix?</summary><p>No. Range alone defines a ring. You also need the assigned radial and altitude.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Is platform only an altitude?</summary><p>No. In the source profile it is a reporting point normally at 5,000 ft and about 20 NM from the ship, where the descent rate reduces to 2,000 ft/min.</p></details>
 <details><summary>When should Marshal hand you to Approach?</summary><p>During the penetration, ideally before platform. Check in on the new button with your fuel state.</p></details>
 <details><summary>How long does a 90° turn take at one F-14 needle width?</summary><p>One width is 360° in four minutes, 1.5° per second, so 90° takes 60 seconds. The marshal pattern's 180° turns take two minutes at the same rate.</p></details>
 <details><summary>What does a ball below the datum lights mean?</summary><p>You are below the glidepath. A red lens means dangerously low; waveoff lights mean wave off immediately.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Does a bolter mean you can join the Case I visual pattern?</summary><p>No. Follow the applicable Case III bolter procedure and controller instructions.</p></details>
