 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <p id="source-p816"><strong>[1] Naval Air Training Command, CNATRA P-816 (01-14), CV Procedures (UMFO), T-45C.</strong> Revision 28 January 2014, Change 1 dated 9 February 2018. <a href="https://murphysys.com/dcs/P-816.pdf">Read the Navy-authored publication (third-party mirror)</a>. Inspected 24 October 2026: LSOs p. 1-2; CCA p. 1-3; TACAN, ACLS and ICLS terms pp. 1-9–1-10; optical landing system pp. 1-11–1-13; §204, pp. 2-11–2-12 and 2-19–2-27; weather criteria pp. 2-3–2-4; waveoff, Charlie and ball calls pp. 2-17–2-18; glossary A-1–A-3 and A-5 (Platform). Historical training context, not evidence of current fleet-wide applicability. Original paraphrases and geometry only; no manual files or figures are redistributed.</p>
 <p id="source-general"><strong>[2] US Navy, NAVEDTRA 14342A, Air Traffic Controller, chapter 11, Shipboard Operations, pp. 11-2, 11-21, 11-32–11-33.</strong> <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/Aircraft-Recovery-Information-388.html">Read the Navy-authored general carrier-control passage (HTML mirror)</a>. Supports control roles, clearance fields, type-dependent marshal fix, BRC/final-bearing definitions and a jet/turboprop marshal example; it does not establish aircraft-specific handling or current operational authority.</p>
 <p id="source-aim"><strong>[3] Federal Aviation Administration, Aeronautical Information Manual, chapter 5, section 3, paragraph 5-3-8 “Holding”.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap5_section_3.html">Read the AIM section (faa.gov)</a>. Inspected 24 October 2026: entry procedures 5-3-8 j 3 (a)–(e), including the nonstandard-pattern note; holding turn rate and bank 5-3-8 j 8. US civil instrument holding, cited for a general instrument technique (the entries and the entry turn rate in the practice) and a comparison rate; it does not establish Navy marshal procedure.</p>
 <p id="source-heatblur"><strong>[4] Heatblur Simulations, DCS: F-14 Tomcat manual, “Center Panel”, Turn-and-Slip Indicator.</strong> <a href="https://f14.manuals.heatblur.se/f14ab/cockpit/pilot/center_panel.html">Read the manual page (heatblur.se)</a>; source text at <a href="https://github.com/Heatblur-Simulations/f-14-manual/blob/49bbd9a144c32033bd3b3d98f04b113c58f22fa0/src/f14bu/cockpit/pilot/center_panel.md">commit 49bbd9a</a>. Inspected 24 October 2026: one needle deflection corresponds to a 360° turn in four minutes. Simulator-module documentation for the DCS F-14, not the aircraft's NATOPS; module version not verified.</p>
 <p>Page-specific source excerpts and provenance are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/case-iii-p816-evidence.md">P-816 evidence</a> and <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/case-iii-general-source.md">general carrier-control evidence</a> and <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/case-iii-visual-module-evidence.md">figure, communications and turn-needle evidence</a>. The module's conceptual structure is aircraft-agnostic; published profile numbers, reporting examples and handling sequences remain attributed to their documented contexts.</p>
 <p class="note"><strong>Source conflict:</strong> this edition gives different Case III visibility thresholds in its weather-criteria section (p. 2-4) and recovery section (p. 2-22). Exact weather minima are therefore unresolved here. This module teaches the night/instrument-recovery purpose without asserting a numerical threshold. It remains Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
 <p>Figures 1–5 are original line drawings, not reproductions of any manual figure. They are not to scale; every label repeats a value stated in the text beside it with its source. The profile, stack and turn practice use the simplified models described with them.</p>
 <p>The geometry interaction is a simplified educational model: flat station-centred coordinates, no wind, horizontal range and a fixed 250 NM/hour illustration rate. Its equations are shown with the worked example. This is not an IAS-to-groundspeed conversion or a prescribed approach.</p>
 <p>The module's source review is separate from the trainer's validation. Neither the T-45C publication nor this module validates the trainer's Tomcat gates, radio script or scoring.</p>
